§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses™„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙhAnd the LORD stirred up an adversary unto Solomon, Hadad the Edomite: he was of the king's seed in Edom.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ•For it came to pass, when David was in Edom, and Joab the captain of the host was gone up to bury the slain, after he had smitten every male in Edom;¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ`(for six months did Joab remain there with all Israel, until he had cut off every male in Edom:)¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ}that Hadad fled, he and certain Edomites of his father's servants with him, to go into Egypt; Hadad being yet a little child.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÖAnd they arose out of Midian, and came to Paran: and they took men with them out of Paran, and they came to Egypt, unto Pharaoh king of Egypt; which gave him an house, and appointed him victuals, and gave him land.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙAnd Hadad found great favour in the sight of Pharaoh, so that he gave him to wife the sister of his own wife, the sister of Tahpenes the queen.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¡And the sister of Tahpenes bare him Genubath his son, whom Tahpenes weaned in Pharaoh's house: and Genubath was in Pharaoh's household among the sons of Pharaoh.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¹And when Hadad heard in Egypt that David slept with his fathers, and that Joab the captain of the host was dead, Hadad said to Pharaoh, Let me depart, that I may go to mine own country.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ®Then Pharaoh said unto him, But what hast thou lacked with me, that, behold, thou seekest to go to thine own country? And he answered, Nothing: howbeit let me go in any wise.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book
§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙuAnd God stirred him up another adversary, Rezon the son of Eliadah, which fled from his lord Hadadezer king of Zobah:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ£and he gathered men unto him, and became captain over a band, when David slew them of Zobah: and they went to Damascus, and dwelt therein, and reigned in Damascus.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙAnd he was an adversary to Israel all the days of Solomon, beside the mischief that Hadad did: and he abhorred Israel, and reigned over Syria.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book
§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses“„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¦And Jeroboam the son of Nebat, an Ephrathite of Zereda, Solomon's servant, whose mother's name was Zeruah, a widow woman, even he lifted up his hand against the king.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ“And this was the cause that he lifted up his hand against the king: Solomon built Millo, and repaired the breaches of the city of David his father.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¨And the man Jeroboam was a mighty man of valour: and Solomon seeing the young man that he was industrious, he made him ruler over all the charge of the house of Joseph.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book
§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse'¦verses›„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÓAnd it came to pass at that time when Jeroboam went out of Jerusalem, that the prophet Ahijah the Shilonite found him in the way; and he had clad himself with a new garment; and they two were alone in the field:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙPand Ahijah caught the new garment that was on him, and rent it in twelve pieces:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¸and he said to Jeroboam, Take thee ten pieces: for thus saith the LORD, the God of Israel, Behold, I will rend the kingdom out of the hand of Solomon, and will give ten tribes to thee:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse ¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ’(but he shall have one tribe for my servant David's sake, and for Jerusalem's sake, the city which I have chosen out of all the tribes of Israel:)¦layout ªlast_verse „¥verse!¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÚ8because that they have forsaken me, and have worshipped Ashtoreth the goddess of the Zidonians, Chemosh the god of the Moabites, and Milcom the god of the children of Ammon, and have not walked in my ways, to do that which is right in mine eyes, and to keep my statutes and my judgments, as did David his father.¦layout ªlast_verse!„¥verse"¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÊHowbeit I will not take the whole kingdom out of his hand: but I will make him prince all the days of his life for David my servant's sake, whom I chose, because he kept my commandments and my statutes:¦layout ªlast_verse"„¥verse#¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ_but I will take the kingdom out of his son's hand, and will give it unto thee, even ten tribes.¦layout ªlast_verse#„¥verse$¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¢And unto his son will I give one tribe, that David my servant may have a light alway before me in Jerusalem, the city which I have chosen me to put my name there.¦layout ªlast_verse$„¥verse%¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙrAnd I will take thee, and thou shalt reign according to all that thy soul desireth, and shalt be king over Israel.¦layout ªlast_verse%„¥verse&¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÚ.And it shall be, if thou wilt hearken unto all that I command thee, and wilt walk in my ways, and do that is right in my sight, to keep my statutes and my commandments, as David my servant did; that I will be with thee, and build thee a sure house, as I built for David, and will give Israel unto thee.¦layout ªlast_verse&„¥verse'¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ@And I will for this afflict the seed of David, but not for ever.¦layout ªlast_verse'…¤book
